  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Writing the New World: The Politics of Natural History in the Early Spanish Empire (2021), published by University of Florida Press, has been cited 10 times \u2014 enough that OpenAlex puts it in the top 10% of works in its field and year. Beyond that, it has been downloaded 1,371 times.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Writing the New World: The Politics of Natural History in the Early Spanish Empire by Mauro Jose Caraccioli was published by University of Florida Press in 2021. Conceptually, the work connects to Politics, Modernity, Empire, Colonialism and Narrative. The book was recognised: ACLS Open Access Book Prize & Arcadia Open Access Publishing Award finalist (2026) \u2014 Environmental Humanities.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">OpenAlex records 10 citations. That places it in the top 10% of works in its field and year of publication. Citations peaked in 2022 with 2 citing works. Notable citing venues include Citizenship Studies, Fronteras de la historia and Librosdelacorte es. The citing works include 5 journal articles and 5 book chapters. They come from 4 countries, led by the United States, Switzerland and Italy.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">It has been downloaded 1,371 times via Figshare and Ubiquity Press. It is recorded in 4 library collections. Its subject matter aligns with Life in Land among the Sustainable Development Goals.</p>",
