  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Writing the New World has shown clear scholarly uptake and strong use in the field. It has 10 OpenAlex citations, placing it in the top 10% of cited works for its year and field, and it has also been downloaded 1,371 times. The book\u2019s citation trail points to continuing engagement across history, philosophy, colonial studies, and environmental humanities.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Mauro J. Caraccioli\u2019s Writing the New World is gaining recognition as a consequential contribution to scholarship on colonial Latin America, natural history, and political thought. The strongest signal in the record is academic uptake: the book has 10 citations in OpenAlex and is ranked in the top 10% of cited works for its field and publication year. That pattern suggests the book is not only being noticed, but is being used as a source in subsequent research.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing literature shows a broad and interdisciplinary reach. Recent works citing the book appear in articles and book chapters across venues in history of historiography, Latin American studies, environmental politics, philosophy, and related humanities fields. This is consistent with the book\u2019s central argument that early Spanish natural history writing helped shape modern political thought through colonial forms of narrative, wonder, and order-making.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage indicators reinforce that scholarly interest. The book has recorded 1,371 downloads, indicating active reading and discovery over time. It is also present in library holdings and subject classifications that connect it to biology, natural history, political science, philosophy, and political aspects, which suggests that it is finding an audience across disciplinary boundaries.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record also points to some thematic policy and sustainability relevance, especially through its alignment with Life in Land. That connection fits the book\u2019s engagement with colonial natural history and environmental knowledge, although the available evidence is strongest for academic rather than public-policy uptake. Public event data were not available in the archive, so the impact story here is drawn mainly from citations, downloads, and library reach.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, Writing the New World appears to be an emerging reference point for scholars thinking about empire, colonialism, narrative, and the history of ideas. Its citation profile, sustained downloads, and cross-field use indicate that the book is contributing to ongoing debates about how early modern natural history helped organize knowledge about the New World and its peoples.</p>",
